From: Wine Fox
Subject: [PATCH] winewayland: Drags out of floating windows as drags the compositor runs.

Wayland does not tell where toplevels are: a floating window the compositor moved is not where
Wine thinks. While a button is down on it, the pointer is in its coordinates wherever it goes,
off by as much over the other windows: a tab Unreal Engine drags out of a floating window moved
away from the centre stopped at invisible borders, and could not be docked where the pointer was.
Wine learnt where the window is only when the pointer went straight from one window into another.

Such a drag now goes on as a drag the compositor runs (wl_data_device, version 3), the
application's decorator its image, which the compositor keeps at the pointer. It starts when an
unowned click-through popup (Unreal's decorator of a dragged tab or asset) is shown while a button
is down on another of our toplevels, not maximized nor fullscreen. The compositor tells over which
of our windows the drag is and where: the pointer gets there in Wine, a straight crossing from the
window it started from aligns that window, and the button is released on the drop, or on the
cancel when it ends elsewhere (the pointer then just past the edge of the window it left).

The drag's source is of a data device manager of version 3, bound for it: the clipboard's is bound
at version 2, whose sources are not told of a drop on nothing. Its events come to a data device of
its own, or to the clipboard's: KWin sends a client's drags to its first data device only (the
clipboard's, when it does not use data control); version 2 offers take the copy action.

The pointer's motions over our windows are sent at once (a drag has no wl_pointer.frame: the
cursor stood still for the application, which showed no docking preview). The image is moved from
the pointer as the application moves its window from the cursor (Unreal's tab over a place to dock
it and back), with its next buffer.

